<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Civil war era in Norway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1130">1130</a>–<a href="/years/1240">1240</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The civil war era in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> was a period of intermittent wars between rival claimants to the Norwegian throne from 1130 to 1240. With no fixed succession law, any son of a king could claim the crown, and factions such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birkebeiner">Birkebeiner</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagler">Bagler</a> fought for power. It ended when Haakon IV defeated Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skule-bardsson">Skule Bårdsson</a> in 1240, after which the monarchy was strengthened.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/civil-war-era-in-norway.jpg" alt="Civil war era in Norway" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Nicolai Arbo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sverrir_by_Arbo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Co-rule had long been the usual answer when two or more worthy candidates existed, and the main test of a claim was descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a> in the male line, legitimate or not. In the late 1120s Harald Gille arrived from Ireland claiming to be a son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-barefoot">Magnus Barefoot</a>, proved it by ordeal of fire and swore not to claim the title while King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigurd-the-crusader">Sigurd the Crusader</a> or his son Magnus lived. When Sigurd died in 1130 he broke the oath, and some two dozen kings and pretenders would fight over the throne in the following century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Harald captured Magnus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> in 1135 and had him blinded and mutilated; Sigurd Slembe, another claimant, murdered Harald in 1136 and was defeated at Holmengrå in 1139. Harald's sons later fell out: Inge Crouchback's men killed Sigurd Munn in 1155, and Øystein Haraldsson was murdered in 1157. His followers then chose a ten-year-old figurehead, Håkon the Broadshouldered, a sign that factions now outlived their leaders. Inge's party answered in 1161 with the five-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-erlingsson">Magnus Erlingsson</a>, whose father Erling Skakke allied with the Church; Magnus became the first crowned king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in 1163, under a written law favouring the eldest legitimate son.</p><p>From 1174 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birkebeiner">Birkebeiner</a>, named for the birch bark some wore as footwear, rose against Magnus. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sverre-of-norway">Sverre Sigurdsson</a>, whose royal descent most historians doubt, they killed Erling at Kalvskinnet in 1179 and Magnus at Fimreite in 1184. The Church opposed Sverre, and in 1198 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> under interdict. In 1197 his opponents formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagler">Bagler</a>, named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s crosier. A settlement at Kvitsøy in 1208 left their king Philippus in the east, and in 1217 the Bagler dissolved their party and swore fealty to Håkon Håkonsson.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Contemporaries saw the era as exceptional. Theodoricus the Monk, writing about 1180, ended his history in 1130 rather than record the crimes that followed, and William of Newburgh noted around 1200 that for over a century no king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> had died of age or sickness.</p><p>The sagas present the wars as personal struggles made dangerous by unclear succession and shared kingship. Narve Bjørgo argued that the push towards a unitary kingdom itself triggered them, and Edvard Bull stressed regional rivalries. Older views of a contest between king and aristocracy, and later readings as a class struggle, have lost ground for lack of support in the sources. Knut Helle emphasises the steady growth of royal power, so that the wars can be seen as the final phase of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>'s unification.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Civil war era in Norway” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
